      \field{abstract}{With the help of argument graphs, we study how citing questionable sources impacts publications. As a case study, we examined a computational chemistry protocol that had a code glitch. We present an argument graph that represents the protocol as arguments and the code glitch as an attack. We identified 288 publications that cited the protocol and determined how many were potentially affected by the code glitch using the argument graph. Our findings illustrate a practical application of argument graphs. In future work, we will use argument mining to scale our approach to support knowledge maintenance in digital libraries.}
      \field{journaltitle}{Proceedings of the 13th confrence of the {Ontario} {Society} for the {Study} of {Argumentatio} ({OSSA13})}
